# Python Port Plan — Step 08 · The REPL Loop

## Purpose

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/08_the_repl_loop` to Python, producing
`week1_baseline/python/08_the_repl_loop` that is behaviourally equivalent:
a new `Boukensha.repl` entry point (same DSL-block wiring as `Boukensha.run`,
minus `task:`) backed by a new `Repl` class that loops reading stdin,
running one agent turn per input line, and printing the reply — with
built-in `/quiet`, `/loud`, `/clear`, `/help`, `/exit`/`/quit` commands and
conversation history that now persists across turns (a real behavioural
change to `Agent#run` itself, not just a new wrapper).

This is the ninth entry in the `docs/plans/python_port/<step>` series (see
[`context.md`](context.md) for the index). `07_the_run_dsl` is the direct
predecessor (not yet ported at the time of this writing — see
[`07_the_run_dsl`](07_the_run_dsl)); this step adds `Boukensha::Repl` and
`Boukensha.repl` on top of it, plus a version constant and a config
resolution enhancement.

Confirmed via `diff` against `07_the_run_dsl`: `registry.rb`, `tool.rb`,
`message.rb`, `prompt_builder.rb`, `run_dsl.rb`, `logger.rb`, `errors.rb`,
`tasks/*.rb`, `backends/*.rb` are **byte-identical**. `context.rb` only
loses its trailing newline (cosmetic). `agent.rb`, `client.rb`, `config.rb`,
and `boukensha.rb` each gain a real, meaningful change; two new files
(`repl.rb`, `version.rb`) are added.

